    "result_text": "All done. The build is green: **Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned the identity key and duplicate-detection logic per the spec.\n\n**`Owner.java`**\n- Rewrote `identityKey` to be the full lower-case **SHA-256 hex** over `normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName)` (null components rendered as empty string). Dropped the `postcode` parameter \u2014 it's no longer part of identity. The result is a 64-char hex string, matching the test's `[0-9a-f]{64}` oracle.\n- Added a standard American **`soundex(String)`** helper (first letter + 3 digit codes, vowels separate, `H`/`W` ignored, adjacent same-codes collapse, zero-padded to 4).\n\n**`ClinicServiceImpl.java`**\n- `assertIdentityIsUnique` now rejects (409) when the new owner's **whole identityKey** equals an existing non-deleted owner's key \u2014 the single duplicate rule. The email-domain blocklist still runs first (unchanged ordering in `saveOwner`).\n- `assignPossibleDuplicate` (soft match) now triggers when the **identityKey differs** but **`soundex(lastName)` and `postcode` both match** an existing non-deleted owner (lowest id wins). This means two owners with the same last name + postcode but different telephones are now a soft match, not a 409.\n- The separate household-duplicate 409 was already gone (no `assertHouseholdIsUnique` existed); I updated the stale javadoc accordingly. `householdId` is retained where it's still legitimately used \u2014 household member counts, the membership-level ceiling, and the response `householdId` field.\n\n**Supporting updates:** `OwnerMapper.formatIdentityKey` (3-arg call + doc), `openapi.yml` identityKey schema (new description, `^[0-9a-f]{64}$` pattern, hex example), and the javadoc on `DuplicateIdentityException` / `ExceptionControllerAdvice`.\n\nThe acceptance tests confirm both behaviours: `coreIdentityKeyIsSha256Hex` (64-hex shape) and `coreRejectsDuplicateIdentity` (identical identity \u2192 409, visible in the log as a 64-hex key collision).",
